<!--

    Copyright (C) 2026 Antony Stubbs and contributors

    THE DEMO'S LOG LEVELS, AND WHY THE DEMO NEEDS ITS OWN.

    With no configuration at all, logback defaults to DEBUG on a console appender - which for this
    demo means the engine, the gRPC stack and the Kafka clients bury the two tables the reader came
    for. The demo therefore prints its own output with println (see Console) and turns the
    libraries down to WARN here, so the product of the run is legible whatever the reader's
    terminal is doing.

    Testcontainers stays at INFO deliberately: pulling a broker image is the longest wait in a
    first run, and a reader staring at a silent terminal cannot tell it from a hang.

    THE ODD FILENAME IS THE WHOLE POINT, AND IT IS A BUG FIX RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE. This demo's
    classpath carries parallel-consumer-core's test output, which contains a logback-test.xml -
    and logback looks for logback-test.xml BEFORE logback.xml, whatever the classpath order. Named
    logback.xml this file was silently ignored and the first run printed core's INFO configuration
    instead. So it carries a name nothing else can claim, and run.sh and the Dockerfile both name
    it in -Dlogback.configurationFile, which beats both defaults outright.

    Consequently it does NOT reach the spawned sidecar: the client library hands the child this
    JVM's classpath, but not its system properties. That is deliberate here - the sidecar's stdout
    and stderr are drained by Sidecar.kt and logged at debug, so its own levels never reach this
    terminal anyway.

-->
<configuration>
    <appender name="STDOUT" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder>
            <pattern>%d{HH:mm:ss.SSS} %-5level %logger{28} - %msg%n</pattern>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <logger name="org.testcontainers" level="INFO"/>
    <logger name="tc" level="INFO"/>

    <root level="WARN">
        <appender-ref ref="STDOUT"/>
    </root>
</configuration>
